<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bougainville Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bougainville is the largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Islands chain but is politically part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. Named after the French navigator Louis-Antoine de Bougainville, it was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, and saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Grievances over the Panguna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine led to a civil war from 1988 to 1998, and in a 2019 referendum its people voted overwhelmingly for independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bougainville-island.jpg" alt="Bougainville Island" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: user:Electionworld · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BougainvilleBukaandNeighbourhood.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
